<section class="net-reading" aria-labelledby="net-about-exam">
  <h2 id="net-about-exam">What is UGC-NET?</h2>
  <p>The University Grants Commission National Eligibility Test (UGC-NET) is a national test. The National Testing Agency (NTA) conducts it for the University Grants Commission (UGC). It decides whether an Indian national is eligible for the Junior Research Fellowship (JRF), for appointment as an Assistant Professor, or for admission to a Ph.D. programme in Indian universities and colleges.</p>
  <p>For English Literature, choose <strong>English, subject code 30</strong>. Paper 1 is the same for every subject. Paper 2 tests the subject you choose.</p>

  <dl class="net-facts">
    <div><dt>Conducted by</dt><dd>National Testing Agency (NTA) for the UGC</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Mode</dt><dd>Computer-based test</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Papers</dt><dd>2 papers, 150 questions, 300 marks</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Time</dt><dd>3 hours, with no break</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Marking</dt><dd>2 marks for each correct answer. No negative marking.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>English subject code</dt><dd>30</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <h3>What your result can qualify you for</h3>
  <p>Your result places you in one of three categories.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>The three qualifying categories</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">You qualify for</th><th scope="col">JRF</th><th scope="col">Assistant Professor</th><th scope="col">Ph.D. admission</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Category 1: Award of JRF and appointment as Assistant Professor</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Category 2: Appointment as Assistant Professor and admission to Ph.D.</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Category 3: Admission to Ph.D. only</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>NTA publishes a separate Information Bulletin for each exam cycle. The facts on this page come from the bulletin for the December 2026 cycle. Rules and dates can change in a new cycle, so read the current bulletin before you apply.</p>
  <div class="net-detail-links"><a href="/exams/net/syllabus/">Explore the NET syllabus<img src="/assets/icons/feather/arrow-right.svg" alt="" width="20" height="20"></a></div>
</section>

<section class="net-reading net-anchor" id="net-about-eligibility" aria-labelledby="net-about-eligibility-h">
  <h2 id="net-about-eligibility-h">Who can apply? Eligibility</h2>
  <p>UGC-NET is for Indian nationals. The marks you need depend on your qualifying degree and your category.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Minimum marks in the qualifying degree</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Qualifying degree</th><th scope="col">Who</th><th scope="col">Minimum marks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row" rowspan="2">Master’s degree or equivalent, from a university or institution recognised by the UGC</th><td>General / Unreserved and General-EWS</td><td><strong>55%</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>OBC-NCL, SC, ST, PwD / PwBD and Third gender</td><td><strong>50%</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Master’s degree completed by 19 September 1991</th><td>Ph.D. holders</td><td><strong>50%</strong>, which is a 5% relaxation from 55%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Four-year (8-semester) bachelor’s degree</th><td>All candidates</td><td><strong>75%</strong> in aggregate, or the equal grade. A 5% relaxation may be allowed for SC, ST, OBC-NCL, differently-abled, EWS and other categories, as the UGC decides.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>For the Master’s degree, marks are counted without rounding off.</p>

  <h3>Important points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Subject.</strong> You are advised to appear in the subject of your Master’s degree. If your subject is not in the NET list, you may appear in a related subject.</li>
    <li><strong>Final-year students and delayed results.</strong> You may apply while you are studying for your Master’s degree, or while your result is awaited. You are admitted provisionally. You become eligible for JRF or Assistant Professor only after you pass with the required marks (55%, or 50% for the reserved categories above). You must do this within two years of the NET result, or within one year if you qualify for Ph.D. admission only. If you do not, you are treated as disqualified.</li>
    <li><strong>Four-year bachelor’s degree.</strong> A NET result on this basis gives eligibility for JRF and Ph.D. admission. It does <strong>not</strong> give eligibility for Assistant Professor. You may take any subject on the NET list, whatever your bachelor’s subject was. Students in the last semester or year may apply provisionally, under the UGC Ph.D. Regulations, 2022.</li>
    <li><strong>Third gender.</strong> Candidates get the same relaxation in fee, age and eligibility as SC, ST and PwD / PwBD candidates.</li>
    <li><strong>No documents to send.</strong> You do not send any certificate to NTA, and NTA does not check your details when you register. Your place in the exam is provisional. If you are found ineligible at any stage, your application is cancelled and legal action can follow.</li>
    <li><strong>Other qualifications.</strong> If you hold a foreign qualification, get its equivalence from the UGC. If you hold a postgraduate diploma from an Indian institute, check its equivalence with a Master’s degree through the Association of Indian Universities.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="net-reading net-anchor" id="net-about-age" aria-labelledby="net-about-age-h">
  <h2 id="net-about-age-h">Age limit and relaxation</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Upper age limit by purpose</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Upper age limit</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Junior Research Fellowship (JRF)</th><td>Not more than <strong>30 years</strong> on the 1st day of the month in which the exam ends. For the December 2026 cycle, that date is 1 December 2026.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Assistant Professor</th><td>No upper age limit</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Ph.D. admission</th><td>No upper age limit</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>Age relaxation for JRF</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Relaxation in the JRF age limit</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Ground</th><th scope="col">Relaxation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">OBC-NCL, SC, ST, PwD / PwBD, Third gender, and women</th><td>Up to 5 years</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Research experience</th><td>The time spent on research in your subject or a related subject, up to a maximum of 5 years. You need a certificate from the proper authority. Research done for a graduation or postgraduation degree does not count.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">LL.M. degree</th><td>3 years</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Service in the armed forces</th><td>Up to 5 years, based on the length of service up to the 1st day of the exam month</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>The total relaxation on these grounds can never be more than 5 years.</strong></p>
</section>

<section class="net-reading net-anchor" id="net-about-pattern" aria-labelledby="net-about-pattern-h">
  <h2 id="net-about-pattern-h">Exam pattern and marking</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Both papers are taken in one computer-based sitting.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Paper</th><th scope="col">What it tests</th><th scope="col">Questions</th><th scope="col">Marks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Paper 1 (Part I)</th><td>Teaching and research aptitude: reasoning, reading comprehension, divergent thinking and general awareness</td><td>50</td><td>100</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Paper 2 (Part II)</th><td>Your chosen subject. For English, this is English Literature (code 30).</td><td>100</td><td>200</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Total</th><td>3 hours (180 minutes), with no break between the papers</td><td>150</td><td>300</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <ul>
    <li>The test is a <strong>computer-based test</strong> only. All questions are objective, multiple-choice questions, and all are compulsory.</li>
    <li>Each question carries <strong>2 marks</strong>. A correct answer earns 2 marks.</li>
    <li>There is <strong>no negative marking</strong> for a wrong answer. A question you do not answer is not marked.</li>
    <li>A question you answered is marked, even if you also marked it for review.</li>
    <li>Question papers are in English and Hindi, except language papers, where the questions are in that language. Choose your medium carefully on the application form. If a translation is unclear, the English version is final.</li>
    <li>The official syllabus is on the UGC website. Our <a href="/exams/net/syllabus/">NET syllabus page</a> lists the units of Paper 1 and of English.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>If a question is faulty</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>How NTA handles questions with problems</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">What is found</th><th scope="col">Who gets 2 marks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">More than one option is correct</th><td>Only those who marked any one of the correct options</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">All options are correct</th><td>All who attempted the question</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">No option is correct, the question is wrong, or it is dropped</th><td>Only those who attempted the question</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<section class="net-reading net-anchor" id="net-about-result" aria-labelledby="net-about-result-h">
  <h2 id="net-about-result-h">How the result is decided</h2>
  <p>The result depends on your <strong>total marks in both papers together</strong>. You must appear in both papers. NTA follows these steps.</p>
  <ol class="net-steps" role="list">
    <li>The number of candidates who qualify for Assistant Professor is <strong>6%</strong> of the candidates who appeared in both papers on the basis of their Master’s degree.</li>
    <li>These places are shared among the categories under the reservation policy of the Government of India.</li>
    <li>To be considered, you must score at least the minimum total marks shown in the table below.</li>
    <li>For each subject and category, the cut-off is the total percentage that matches the number of places for that category.</li>
    <li>The places for JRF are also shared among the categories under the reservation policy.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Minimum total marks to be considered (both papers together)</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Minimum total marks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">General / Unreserved and General-EWS</th><td><strong>40%</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SC, ST, OBC-NCL, PwD / PwBD and Third gender</th><td><strong>35%</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>These are only the minimum marks. The real cut-off is set for each subject and category, so it can be higher. The qualifying criteria decided by the UGC are final.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Marks and percentile.</strong> The result is declared in marks, together with a percentile score. Qualification is decided on marks. The percentile score is not used to decide it.</li>
    <li><strong>Answer key.</strong> NTA shows the provisional answer key and your recorded responses on its website. You can challenge an answer by paying <strong>₹200 for each question</strong>. The fee is not refunded. The result is made from the final answer key.</li>
    <li><strong>No re-checking.</strong> NTA does not re-evaluate or re-check the result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Reservation</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <caption>Reservation of seats in Central Universities and institutions deemed to be universities, as printed in the bulletin</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Share of seats</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Scheduled Caste (SC)</th><td>15%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Scheduled Tribe (ST)</th><td>7.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Other Backward Classes, Non-Creamy Layer (OBC-NCL), Central List</th><td>27%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">General-Economically Weaker Sections (General-EWS)</th><td>10%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Persons with Benchmark Disability (40% or more disability)</th><td>5% of the seats in the categories above</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Choose OBC-NCL only if you are in the Central List of Other Backward Classes. A candidate from a State list who is not in the Central List must choose General / Unreserved.</p>
</section>

<section class="net-reading net-anchor" id="net-about-benefits" aria-labelledby="net-about-benefits-h">
  <h2 id="net-about-benefits-h">Benefits of qualifying in NET</h2>
  <div class="net-cards">
    <article class="net-card">
      <h3>Junior Research Fellowship</h3>
      <p>JRF qualifiers can receive a UGC fellowship under various schemes, once they find a place in a university, IIT or institution. The offer is valid for <strong>three years</strong> from the date of the JRF Award Letter. If you have already joined a Ph.D., the fellowship starts from the date of the result or the date you joined, whichever is later.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="net-card">
      <h3>Assistant Professor eligibility</h3>
      <p>NET, SET or SLET is the minimum eligibility for Assistant Professor in universities, colleges and institutions. Each university, college or State government then follows its own recruitment rules. JRF qualifiers are eligible for Assistant Professor too.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="net-card">
      <h3>Ph.D. admission</h3>
      <p>A JRF qualifier is admitted to a Ph.D. through an interview, under the UGC Ph.D. Regulations, 2022. For Category 2 and Category 3 qualifiers, NET can replace a university’s own entrance test. Then <strong>70%</strong> of the merit comes from NET marks and <strong>30%</strong> from the interview. These NET marks are valid for <strong>one year</strong> from the date of the result.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="net-card">
      <h3>Research placement</h3>
      <p>JRF qualifiers may do research in the subject of their Master’s degree or a related subject. Universities, institutions, IITs and other national organisations may select JRF awardees for full-time research, using their own procedure.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="net-card">
      <h3>National fellowships</h3>
      <p>Selection for the National Fellowship for Scheduled Caste Students, the National Fellowship for Other Backward Classes and the National Fellowship for Persons with Disabilities is made through the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment. Each follows its own ministry’s guidelines.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="net-card">
      <h3>Wider use of your result</h3>
      <p>The UGC and NTA can share NET candidate data with higher education institutions for Ph.D. admission. They can also share it with public sector undertakings and other organisations for recruitment.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="net-callout">
    <p><strong>Exemptions from NET for Assistant Professor.</strong> The UGC regulations decide who is exempt. The bulletin also names two groups: candidates who passed the UGC or CSIR JRF examination before 1989, and candidates who cleared a State Eligibility Test (SET) before 1 December 2002. A SET cleared from 1 December 2002 onwards is valid only in the State where the candidate cleared it.</p>
    <p><strong>Good to know.</strong> The bulletin does not fix the amount of the JRF fellowship. It depends on the scheme that applies when you are placed. Check the UGC and your university for the current amount.</p>
  </div>
</section>

